<p>
  South Florida's lizards come mostly from the tropics, and every one of them has
  a lower limit: a body temperature at which it can no longer turn itself the right
  way up, known as its critical thermal minimum. On 22 January 2020, air temperatures in Miami fell
  below the limits of the lizards living there.
</p>
<p>
  We happened to be ready for it. In October and November 2016 we had measured
  the cold tolerance of six lizard species living together in Miami: four anoles
  (the <a href="/miami-lizards/green-anole/">green</a>,
  <a href="/miami-lizards/brown-anole/">brown</a>,
  <a href="/miami-lizards/crested-anole/">crested</a> and
  <a href="/miami-lizards/bark-anole/">bark</a> anoles), the brown basilisk and the
  African house gecko. They differ in size, habitat and physiology, and before the
  cold snap their cold limits differed too.
</p>

<h2>Six species, one new limit</h2>
<p>
  In the week after the freeze we measured all six species again. As a community
  they could now tolerate colder temperatures, and species that had differed
  beforehand had converged on the same new, lower limit, averaging about 7.2°C.
  Ten weeks later the new limits were still in place in the five species we could
  resample (COVID-19 curfews ruled out night sampling for the house gecko).
</p>
<p>
  How does a lizard's cold tolerance change so fast? Two explanations are
  possible, and they are not mutually exclusive. The cold may have killed the least
  tolerant individuals, leaving a population shaped by selection, or the survivors
  may have adjusted their physiology in response to the cold itself. Either way,
  the result cuts against a common assumption: tropical and subtropical cold-blooded
  animals are often thought unable to withstand rapid swings in climate, yet these
  lizards endured conditions beyond their measured physiological limits.
</p>

<h2>Back out in the cold</h2>
<p>
  Extreme cold events keep coming. When another hit Miami in February 2026, the lab
  was out at 4am, in winter hats and head torches, ready to sample cold-stunned
  lizards. A study with colleagues on how lizards lose, and then recover, the
  ability to move after cold exposure is currently in review. It connects to our
  research on green anoles further north, where surviving winter is the central
  challenge for a lizard of tropical origin (see the
  <a href="/miami-lizards/green-anole/">green anole guide</a>).
</p>
<p>
  The 2020 study, led by James Stroud and Caitlin Mothes, was published in
  <em>Biology Letters</em>.
</p>
